The current enshrined deity, according to the Shrine Register,<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref> is:
* {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大国主|lt=Ōkuninushi|lt_ja=大己貴神}}

In the "Yamato Shiryō" the deity is regarded as the spirit of the prefecture, with some theories also including Amatsu {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

The term “{{ill|Prefecture Lord|ja|県主|lt=Prefecture Lord|lt_ja=県}} (agata)” appearing in the shrine’s name refers to land under the direct control of the ancient court (kugoryo territory). The six prefectures of {{ill|Yamato Province|ja|大和国}} – Takaichi, Katsuragi, Juichi, Shiki, Yamabe, and Sofu – had especially close ties with the court. Collectively known as {{ill|Mutsunomiagata|ja|倭の六県|lt=Mutsunomiagata|lt_ja=六御県}} (read “mutsunomiagata”), each prefecture had its own mikata shrine, with this shrine being one among them.<ref>{{ill|Ueda Masaaki|ja|上田正昭}} "Rokugoken" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}. The head of the prefecture where this shrine is enshrined is '''Isoji Kengunushi''' (also known as Shiki Kengunushi or Shiki no Agatanushi, later Shikiren). In the {{ill|Shinsen Seishiroku|ja|新撰姓氏録}} of Yamato Province, the deity is identified as a descendant of Hikoyudenshin (the grandson of {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命}}), while in the "Tenson Hongi" of {{ill|Sendai Kojihonki|ja|先代旧事本紀}} he is described as a descendant of either Kenshinkawa-no-mikoto (the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito) or of Mononobe Inabamiren-kō (the child of Juichi-ne no Mikoto, the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito).<ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Furthermore, in the 2nd year entry for {{ill|Emperor Jimmu|ja|神武天皇}} in {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀}}, it appears that the warrior {{ill|Otoko Isoji|ja|弟磯城}} – active during the {{ill|Jimmu Eastern Expedition|ja|神武東征}} – was designated Isoji Kengunushi.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Another theory holds that Isoji Kengunushi is a descendant of {{ill|Kotoshiro-nushi|ja|事代主神}}, with past scholar {{ill|Ota Ryo|ja|太田亮}} having remarked that “Isoji no Oji is none other than the {{ill|Miwa clan|ja|三輪氏}}.”<ref>{{ill|Takaraga Hisao|ja|宝賀寿男}} "Naganarihiko and the Genealogy of Isoji Kengunushi" in ''Miwa-shi: Worshippers of Ōmononushi'', Aogaki Publishing, 2015</ref>. If this theory is adopted, it would also explain why the shrine enshrines Ōkuninushi.

==History==
===General History===
The founding date is unknown.

In the 2nd year of {{ill|Tenpyo|ja|天平}} (730), the "Yamato Province Shōzei Chō" records list “Shiki no Mikata” as a divine gate ({{ill|Kamibe|ja|神戸 (民戸)|lt=Kamibe|lt_ja=神戸}}) drawn from within {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡}}, with a total allotment of 1,351 bundles and 8 units of tax rice – of which 4 bundles were allocated for the enshrinement fee.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 1st year of {{ill|Daido|ja|大同}} (806) according to the imperial edict recorded in {{ill|Shinshō Kakuchofu-sho|ja|新抄格勅符抄}}, 12 households from {{ill|Yamato Province|ja|大和国}} were assigned as the divine gate for “Shiki no Mikata God”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

According to the history in {{ill|Rokukokushi|ja|六国史|lt=National History|lt_ja=国史}}, in the 3rd year of {{ill|Tenan|ja|天安 (日本)|lt=Tenan|lt_ja=天安}} (859) the divine rank ({{ill|Shinkai|ja|神階}}) of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 5th year of {{ill|Enchō|ja|延長 (元号)|lt=Enchō}} (927), as recorded in the established {{ill|Engishiki Jinmyōchō|ja|延喜式神名帳|lt=Jinmyōchō|lt_ja=神名帳}}, the shrine – listed as “Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine, Great Tsukiji Niinamesai” – was placed among the {{ill|Shikinai-taisha|ja|式内大社}} in {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡}}, with a note that offerings of cloth and ritual paper were received at the court’s monthly and Niinamesai festivals.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 6th year of {{ill|Bunmei|ja|文明}} (1474), the "Shukuin-kai Beichō" makes a reference to “Shikinomiya”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

After the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新}}, under the modern shrine ranking system ({{ill|Modern Shrine Ranking System|ja|近代社格制度}}) the shrine was classified as a {{ill|Sonsha|ja|村社}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

===Divine Rank===
* In the 3rd year of {{ill|Tenan|ja|天安 (日本)|lt=Tenan|lt_ja=天安}} (859), on {{ill|January 27|ja|1月27日}}, the divine rank of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper (as recorded in {{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|ja|日本三代実録}}) – the inscription reads “志貴御県神”.

==Shrine Grounds==
The area surrounding Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine is, according to one theory, identified with the site of {{ill|Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|ja|磯城瑞籬宮|lt=Isoji Mizurubashii Palace}} (also known as Shiki Mizugaki Palace), which is mentioned in both {{ill|Kojiki|ja|古事記}} and {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀}} as having been established by the 10th-generation {{ill|Emperor Sujin|ja|崇神天皇}}. A stone monument now stands on the site.<ref>{{ill|Kawaguchi Katsuyasu|ja|川口勝康}} "Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref><ref>"Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Nihon Rekishi Chimei Taikei 30: Place Names of Nara Prefecture|ja|日本歴史地名大系 30 奈良県の地名}}, Heibonsha, 1981.</ref>
