The current enshrined deity, according to the Shrine Register,<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref> is:
* {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大国主|lt=Ōkuninushi|lt_ja=大己貴神|12=draft|12=draft}}

In the "Yamato Shiryō" the deity is regarded as the spirit of the prefecture, with some theories also including Amatsu {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

The term “{{ill|Prefecture Lord|ja|県主|lt=Prefecture Lord|lt_ja=県|12=draft|12=draft}} (agata)” appearing in the shrine’s name refers to land under the direct control of the ancient court (kugoryo territory). The six prefectures of {{ill|Yamato Province|ja|大和国|12=draft|12=draft}} – Takaichi, Katsuragi, Juichi, Shiki, Yamabe, and Sofu – had especially close ties with the court. Collectively known as {{ill|Mutsunomiagata|ja|倭の六県|lt=Mutsunomiagata|lt_ja=六御県|12=draft|12=draft}} (read “mutsunomiagata”), each prefecture had its own mikata shrine, with this shrine being one among them.<ref>{{ill|Ueda Masaaki|ja|上田正昭|12=draft|12=draft}} "Rokugoken" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典|12=draft|12=draft}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}. The head of the prefecture where this shrine is enshrined is '''Isoji Kengunushi''' (also known as Shiki Kengunushi or Shiki no Agatanushi, later Shikiren). In the {{ill|Shinsen Seishiroku|ja|新撰姓氏録|12=draft|12=draft}} of Yamato Province, the deity is identified as a descendant of Hikoyudenshin (the grandson of {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命|12=draft|12=draft}}), while in the "Tenson Hongi" of {{ill|Sendai Kojihonki|ja|先代旧事本紀|12=draft|12=draft}} he is described as a descendant of either Kenshinkawa-no-mikoto (the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito) or of Mononobe Inabamiren-kō (the child of Juichi-ne no Mikoto, the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito).<ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Furthermore, in the 2nd year entry for {{ill|Emperor Jimmu|ja|神武天皇|12=draft|12=draft}} in {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀|12=draft|12=draft}}, it appears that the warrior {{ill|Otoko Isoji|ja|弟磯城|12=draft|12=draft}} – active during the {{ill|Jimmu Eastern Expedition|ja|神武東征|12=draft|12=draft}} – was designated Isoji Kengunushi.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Another theory holds that Isoji Kengunushi is a descendant of {{ill|Kotoshiro-nushi|ja|事代主神|12=draft|12=draft}}, with past scholar {{ill|Ota Ryo|ja|太田亮|12=draft|12=draft}} having remarked that “Isoji no Oji is none other than the {{ill|Miwa clan|ja|三輪氏|12=draft|12=draft}}.”<ref>{{ill|Takaraga Hisao|ja|宝賀寿男|12=draft|12=draft}} "Naganarihiko and the Genealogy of Isoji Kengunushi" in ''Miwa-shi: Worshippers of Ōmononushi'', Aogaki Publishing, 2015</ref>. If this theory is adopted, it would also explain why the shrine enshrines Ōkuninushi.

==History==
===General History===
The founding date is unknown.

In the 2nd year of {{ill|Tenpyo|ja|天平|12=draft|12=draft}} (730), the "Yamato Province Shōzei Chō" records list “Shiki no Mikata” as a divine gate ({{ill|Kamibe|ja|神戸 (民戸)|lt=Kamibe|lt_ja=神戸|12=draft|12=draft}}) drawn from within {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡|12=draft|12=draft}}, with a total allotment of 1,351 bundles and 8 units of tax rice – of which 4 bundles were allocated for the enshrinement fee.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 1st year of {{ill|Daido|ja|大同|12=draft|12=draft}} (806) according to the imperial edict recorded in {{ill|Shinshō Kakuchofu-sho|ja|新抄格勅符抄|12=draft|12=draft}}, 12 households from {{ill|Yamato Province|ja|大和国|12=draft|12=draft}} were assigned as the divine gate for “Shiki no Mikata God”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

According to the history in {{ill|Rokukokushi|ja|六国史|lt=National History|lt_ja=国史|12=draft|12=draft}}, in the 3rd year of {{ill|Tenan|ja|天安 (日本)|lt=Tenan|lt_ja=天安|12=draft|12=draft}} (859) the divine Rank ({{ill|Shinkai (divine Rank)|lt=Shinkai|ja|神階|12=draft|12=draft}}) of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 5th year of {{ill|Enchō|ja|延長 (元号)|lt=Enchō|12=draft|12=draft}} (927), as recorded in the established {{ill|Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|ja|延喜式神名帳|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Jinmyōchō|lt_ja=神名帳|12=draft|12=draft}}, the shrine – listed as “Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine, Great Tsukiji Niinamesai” – was placed among the {{ill|Shikinai-taisha|ja|式内大社|12=draft|12=draft}} in {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡|12=draft|12=draft}}, with a note that offerings of cloth and ritual paper were received at the court’s monthly and Niinamesai festivals.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 6th year of {{ill|Bunmei|ja|文明|12=draft|12=draft}} (1474), the "Shukuin-kai Beichō" makes a reference to “Shikinomiya”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

After the {{ill|Meiji Restoration|ja|明治維新|12=draft|12=draft}}, under the modern shrine Ranking system ({{ill|Modern Shrine Ranking System|ja|近代社格制度|12=draft|12=draft}}) the shrine was classified as a {{ill|Sonsha|ja|村社|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

===Divine Rank===
* In the 3rd year of {{ill|Tenan|ja|天安 (日本)|lt=Tenan|lt_ja=天安|12=draft|12=draft}} (859), on {{ill|January 27|ja|1月27日|12=draft|12=draft}}, the divine Rank of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper (as recorded in {{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|ja|日本三代実録|12=draft|12=draft}}) – the inscription reads “志貴御県神”.

==Shrine Grounds==
The area surrounding Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine is, according to one theory, identified with the site of {{ill|Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|ja|磯城瑞籬宮|lt=Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|12=draft|12=draft}} (also known as Shiki Mizugaki Palace), which is mentioned in both {{ill|Kojiki|ja|古事記|12=draft|12=draft}} and {{ill|Nihon Shoki|ja|日本書紀|12=draft|12=draft}} as having been established by the 10th-generation {{ill|Emperor Sujin|ja|崇神天皇|12=draft|12=draft}}. A stone monument now stands on the site.<ref>{{ill|Kawaguchi Katsuyasu|ja|川口勝康|12=draft|12=draft}} "Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典|12=draft|12=draft}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref><ref>"Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Nihon Rekishi Chimei Taikei 30: Place Names of Nara Prefecture|ja|日本歴史地名大系 30 奈良県の地名|12=draft|12=draft}}, Heibonsha, 1981.</ref>
