The current enshrined deity, according to the Shrine Register,<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref> is:
* [[:en:Ōkuninushi|Ōkuninushi]]

In the "Yamato Shiryō" the deity is regarded as the spirit of the prefecture, with some theories also including Amatsu {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nagasohito|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to ニギハヤヒ}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

The term “[[:en:Agatanushi|Prefecture Lord]] (agata)” appearing in the shrine’s name refers to land under the direct control of the ancient court (kugoryo territory). The six prefectures of [[:en:Yamato Province|Yamato Province]] – Takaichi, Katsuragi, Juichi, Shiki, Yamabe, and Sofu – had especially close ties with the court. Collectively known as {{ill|Mutsunomiagata|ja|倭の六県|lt=Mutsunomiagata|lt_ja=六御県|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mutsunomiagata|qq=}} (read “mutsunomiagata”), each prefecture had its own mikata shrine, with this shrine being one among them.<ref>{{ill|Ueda Masaaki|ja|上田正昭|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ueda Masaaki|qq=}} "Rokugoken" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kokushi Daijiten|qq=}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}. The head of the prefecture where this shrine is enshrined is '''Isoji Kengunushi''' (also known as Shiki Kengunushi or Shiki no Agatanushi, later Shikiren). In the [[:en:Shinsen Shōjiroku|Shinsen Seishiroku]] of Yamato Province, the deity is identified as a descendant of Hikoyudenshin (the grandson of {{ill|Nagasohito|ja|饒速日命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nagasohito|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to ニギハヤヒ}}), while in the "Tenson Hongi" of [[:en:Kujiki|Sendai Kojihonki]] he is described as a descendant of either Kenshinkawa-no-mikoto (the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito) or of Mononobe Inabamiren-kō (the child of Juichi-ne no Mikoto, the seventh-generation descendant of Nagasohito).<ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Furthermore, in the 2nd year entry for [[:en:Emperor Jimmu|Emperor Jimmu]] in [[:en:Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]], it appears that the warrior {{ill|Otoko Isoji|ja|弟磯城|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Otoko Isoji|qq=}} – active during the [[:en:Jimmu's Eastern Expedition|Jimmu Eastern Expedition]] – was designated Isoji Kengunushi.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Isoji Kengunushi (Ancient Clan)|2010}}</ref> Another theory holds that Isoji Kengunushi is a descendant of {{ill|Kotoshiro-nushi|ja|事代主神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kotoshiro-nushi|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 事代主}}, with past scholar {{ill|Ota Ryo|ja|太田亮|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ota Ryo|qq=}} having remarked that “Isoji no Oji is none other than the {{ill|Miwa clan|ja|三輪氏|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Miwa clan|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 大神氏}}.”<ref>{{ill|Takaraga Hisao|ja|宝賀寿男|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takaraga Hisao|qq=}} "Naganarihiko and the Genealogy of Isoji Kengunushi" in ''Miwa-shi: Worshippers of Ōmononushi'', Aogaki Publishing, 2015</ref>. If this theory is adopted, it would also explain why the shrine enshrines Ōkuninushi.

==History==
===General History===
The founding date is unknown.

In the 2nd year of [[:en:Tenpyō|Tenpyo]] (730), the "Yamato Province Shōzei Chō" records list “Shiki no Mikata” as a divine gate ({{ill|Kamibe|ja|神戸 (民戸)|lt=Kamibe|lt_ja=神戸|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kamibe|qq=}}) drawn from within {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jokō District|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 式上郡}}, with a total allotment of 1,351 bundles and 8 units of tax rice – of which 4 bundles were allocated for the enshrinement fee.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 1st year of [[:en:Datong (disambiguation)|Daido]] (806) according to the imperial edict recorded in {{ill|Shinshō Kakuchofu-sho|ja|新抄格勅符抄|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinshō Kakuchofu-sho|qq=}}, 12 households from [[:en:Yamato Province|Yamato Province]] were assigned as the divine gate for “Shiki no Mikata God”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

According to the history in [[:en:Rikkokushi|National History]], in the 3rd year of [[:en:Ten'an|Tenan]] (859) the divine Rank ([[:en:Shinkai (divine rank)|Shinkai]]) of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 5th year of [[:en:Enchō|Enchō]] (927), as recorded in the established {{ill|Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|ja|延喜式神名帳|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|lt=Jinmyōchō|lt_ja=神名帳|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki Jinmyōchō|13=User:Immanuelle/Engishiki Jinmyōchō|qq=}}, the shrine – listed as “Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine, Great Tsukiji Niinamesai” – was placed among the {{ill|Shikinai-taisha|ja|式内大社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinai-taisha|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 名神大社}} in {{ill|Jokō District|ja|城上郡|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Jokō District|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 式上郡}}, with a note that offerings of cloth and ritual paper were received at the court’s monthly and Niinamesai festivals.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

In the 6th year of [[:en:Civilization|Bunmei]] (1474), the "Shukuin-kai Beichō" makes a reference to “Shikinomiya”.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

After the [[:en:Meiji Restoration|Meiji Restoration]], under the modern shrine Ranking system ([[:en:Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines]]) the shrine was classified as a {{ill|Sonsha|ja|村社|1=Son-sha|qid=Q134917284}}{{efn|Former shrine rank|lt=Son-sha|1=Modern_system_of_ranked_Shinto_shrines#Village_shrines|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sonsha|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度}}.<ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (National History)}}</ref><ref>{{Sfn|Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine (Heibonsha)|1981}}</ref>

===Divine Rank===
* In the 3rd year of [[:en:Ten'an|Tenan]] (859), on [[:en:January 27|January 27]], the divine Rank of “Shiki no Mikata God” was raised from Junior Fifth Rank Lower to Junior Fifth Rank Upper (as recorded in [[:en:Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]]) – the inscription reads “志貴御県神”.

==Shrine Grounds==
The area surrounding Shiki no Miagata Nimasu Shrine is, according to one theory, identified with the site of {{ill|Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|ja|磯城瑞籬宮|lt=Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Isoji Mizurubashii Palace|qq=}} (also known as Shiki Mizugaki Palace), which is mentioned in both [[:en:Kojiki|Kojiki]] and [[:en:Nihon Shoki|Nihon Shoki]] as having been established by the 10th-generation [[:en:Emperor Sujin|Emperor Sujin]]. A stone monument now stands on the site.<ref>{{ill|Kawaguchi Katsuyasu|ja|川口勝康|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kawaguchi Katsuyasu|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} "Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Kokushi Daijiten|ja|国史大辞典|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kokushi Daijiten|qq=}}, Yoshikawa Kōbunkan.</ref><ref>"Isoji Mizurubashii Palace" in {{ill|Nihon Rekishi Chimei Taikei 30: Place Names of Nara Prefecture|ja|日本歴史地名大系 30 奈良県の地名|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Nihon Rekishi Chimei Taikei 30: Place Names of Nara Prefecture|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, Heibonsha, 1981.</ref>
